## Ingredients

**Chicken**

- 5 (about 2 lb / 900g) bone-in, skin-on chicken drumsticks
- 1 tsp (3g) kosher salt
- 1/2 tsp (1g) freshly ground black pepper
- 1 tsp (2g) paprika
- 1 tsp (1g) Italian seasoning
- 1 Tbs (14g) olive or avocado oil, for searing (optional)

**Sauce**

- 3 Tbs (about 30g) chopped sun-dried tomatoes, drained if oil-packed (see Notes)
- 4 cloves (12g) garlic, minced
- 1/2 cup (120g) low-sodium chicken broth
- 1 tsp (1g) dried basil or oregano
- 1/4 tsp red pepper flakes (optional)
- 1/2 cup (120g) heavy cream, at room temperature
- 1/3 cup (30g) finely grated parmesan
- Salt, to taste

**To finish**

- Fresh basil, torn or thinly sliced

**To serve**

- Zucchini noodles, cauliflower mash, or roasted broccoli

## Instructions

1. **Season the chicken.** Pat the drumsticks very dry with paper towels. This helps the skin crisp later. Mix the salt, pepper, paprika, and Italian seasoning and rub it all over the drumsticks.
2. **Sear (optional).** For deeper flavor, heat the oil in a large skillet over medium-high heat. Sear the drumsticks 2–3 minutes per side until golden. Skip this step if you're short on time. The broiler at the end will still crisp the skin.
3. **Load the slow cooker.** Scatter the sun-dried tomatoes and garlic in the slow cooker. Pour in the broth and add the dried basil or oregano and the red pepper flakes, if using. Arrange the drumsticks on top in a single layer if you can.
4. **Slow cook.** Cover and cook on Low for 6–7 hours or on High for 3–4 hours, until the chicken is tender and reads at least 175°F (80°C) near the bone.
5. **Add the cream and parmesan.** About 30 minutes before serving, switch the slow cooker to Low if it was on High. Move the drumsticks to a plate. Stir the cream into the cooking liquid, then sprinkle in the parmesan a little at a time, stirring until melted. Return the drumsticks, cover, and cook on Low for the last 30 minutes. Don't let the sauce bubble hard, or the cream can separate.
6. **Crisp the skin.** Heat the broiler to high with a rack about 6 inches from the heat. Line a sheet pan with foil, set the drumsticks on it skin side up, and broil 3–4 minutes, watching closely, until the skin is browned and crisp.
7. **Finish the sauce.** While the chicken broils, taste the sauce and add salt if needed. If it's thinner than you'd like, pour it into a small saucepan and simmer gently for 3–5 minutes to reduce (see Notes).
8. **Serve.** Spoon the sauce over the drumsticks and top with fresh basil. Serve with zucchini noodles, cauliflower mash, or roasted broccoli.

## Notes

- **Nutrition:** Estimated per drumstick with about 1/5 of the sauce, made with oil-packed sun-dried tomatoes (drained), heavy cream, and without the optional searing oil. Doesn't include sides. Net carbs are about 2g per drumstick. One drumstick is a light serving. Most adults will want two, so double the recipe to serve four or five.
- **Why it's low-carb and diabetes-friendly:** Many versions thicken the sauce with flour and add a lot of sun-dried tomatoes. This version skips the flour, and the cream and parmesan give the sauce its body. Sun-dried tomatoes have concentrated natural sugar, so 3 Tbs gives you their flavor without raising the carbs much. The protein and fat from the chicken, cream, and cheese also slow carb absorption from anything you serve on the side.
- **Sides and starches:** Cauliflower mash adds about 5g net carbs per cup, zucchini noodles about 4g, and roasted broccoli about 4g. If you want a starch, keep it to a small portion, like 1/2 cup cooked whole-grain pasta (about 20g carbs) or brown rice (about 22g carbs). Eat the chicken and vegetables first to help keep blood sugar steadier.
- **Sun-dried tomatoes:** Oil-packed tomatoes are softer and richer. Drain them well and pat off the extra oil. Dry-packed tomatoes work too: chop them finely so they soften in the slow cooker.
- **Cream vs. half-and-half:** Heavy cream is the most stable and the lowest in carbs. Half-and-half saves about 40 calories and 5g fat per serving, but it's more likely to split and makes a thinner sauce. If you use it, keep the slow cooker on Low and stir it in gradually.
- **Thicker sauce:** The chicken releases juices as it cooks, so the sauce will be fairly loose. Simmering it on the stove for a few minutes is the easiest way to thicken it. For a thicker sauce without flour, whisk in 1/8 tsp xanthan gum while it simmers. A little goes a long way, so add more only if needed.
- **Skim the fat:** Skin-on drumsticks release fat into the slow cooker. Before adding the cream, tilt the insert and spoon off any clear fat pooled on top.
- **Boneless thighs:** Use about 2 lb (900g) boneless, skinless thighs. Cook on Low for 4–5 hours or High for 2–3 hours, and skip the broiling step.
- **Gluten-free:** Check that your chicken broth is labeled gluten-free.
- **Leftovers:** Refrigerate up to 3 days. Reheat gently on the stove or at reduced power in the microwave so the sauce doesn't separate. Crisp the skin again under the broiler if you like.
